title: Rebuild session-lifetime stores
pid: 50271d91-b3b3-4731-a0be-1a0fe3b51eff
body: |2-
    - Request: ((request . "rebuild session-lifetime stores"))
    - Response: LP response-type "rebuild-session-lifetime-stores" with =((content "..."))=.
      Content reports that the config was reloaded and the graph and
      Tantivy rebuilt on success, followed by one line "Warning: ..." per
      warning of the reload, or "Rebuild failed: ..." on error.
    - Behavior: Re-reads the config file the server started with (an
      edited skgrepo list included), then reads the authoritative =.skg= files once, validates a complete
      candidate graph, builds that graph and a fresh Tantivy index from the same
      node vector, then atomically swaps in the new runtime generation. It does
      not rewrite =.skg= files. Failure, an invalid config included, leaves
      the old generation live. Success rewrites the owned skgrepos'
      =OTHER_SKGREPO_DEPENDENCIES.toml= as a startup does, and warns, as
      a startup does, of unknown config keys, illegal edits to foreign
      nodes and disputed foreign fold orders, and of a changed =port= or
      =timing_log=, which take
      effect only at a restart; the server's startup warnings ("Server
      warnings") stay as they were. Each connection's skgrepo restriction
      follows the new config at its next request: none stays none, and
      a restriction keeps its most private skgrepo shown, or, if the
      config no longer names it as owned, becomes the config's default;
      either way foreign skgrepos stay shown or hidden as they were. Success
      keeps the connection's open views, drops the graph straddle of the
      diff view, and recomputes context rankings for search. A rebuild
      makes no buffer stale by itself: a save is refused only where a
      buffer edits a graphnode whose baseline copy no longer matches the
      rebuilt graph (see "Save
      buffer", the stale buffer). After a successful rebuild the clients
      fetch the herald rules again ("Herald rules"), whose skgrepo labels
      the reloaded config may have changed, and then send "rerender all
      views", which redraws every view without unsaved edits from the
      rebuilt graph, under the reconciled restriction, which each view
      records; a node of a skgrepo the reloaded config no longer names
      is drawn as an unknown one. A view with unsaved edits keeps its
      text and baseline, and is compared at its save like any other.
      Files that
      change on disk need no rebuild: the server reloads them as they
      change ("Repo files reloaded"). A rebuild is what applies a
      changed config.
